A||||9||Brandon86SE||Places to put a sending unit?||||5||
Z||000000||Brandon86SE||09-25-2001||04:26 PM||||Yesterday I was installing a new set of mechanical gauges into the car, but.... The coil connecting the water temp gauge to the sending unit is only 6 feet, which came to be about a foot short!  ARGH! What I'm wondering is if there's a spot to put it other than the factory location?  (top LH side of closest side when standing behind the car).  I'm sure there is, but wasn't able to see anything closer, but my cat was kinda in the way.  If there's something in the engine block on the cabin side of the engine, it would reach.  If anyone knows of a spot it'd be much appreciated!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>     I already checked with the gauge manufacturer, they don't make a longer cable that I could use.  I could go electrical, but if possible I'd like to do mechanical.<P>[Edit] Doesn't have to be in the engine, if you know of a way to get into the rad, or someplace else, that'd be just as helpful!  Oh, and I have the 2.8 and a 4 spd.<P>Thanks, Brandon<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Brandon86SE (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-25-2001||05:57 PM||||ARGH!!! OH GOD STOP ME!!!<P><BR>Bend over and I'll show ya!<P>SORRY!!! Couldn't resist!!!<P>Any location in the head would be fine...<BR>If there is already a sensor in that location, then use a tee fitting to add yours...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-25-2001||06:59 PM||||The heads are identical castings. Therefore, there's a plug in the lower RHS corner of the the forward head (near the battery).  It's just like the hole in the aft head, where the stock sending unit resides.<P>I think that plug in the forward head has a square recess.  Unscrew the plug, replace it with your new sender, and hopefully you're there.<P>When it comes to holes in the head, I ought to know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Hope it works for you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brandon86SE||09-26-2001||07:02 PM||||Ok, I just pulled myself out from under my car, and I couldn't see the spot by the battery where the second spot for my water temp sending unit is supposed to be.  Does anyone happen to have a pic of the underbelly of the 2.8 that can point it out to me.  I'm still kinda new to this backyard mechanic thing, so I'm sure it's there, and plugged and I just missed it, but it'd be nice to see exactly what I'm supposed to be looking for.  Thanks,<P>Brandon||||reg||5
Z||000004||Leadfoot||09-26-2001||08:31 PM||||You won't be able to see it from under the car.  Stand above the engine and look at the area where the dip stick bolts to the engine.  You will see a bracket that the neg side of the battery bolts to (actually it is held by a stud that bolts the bracket to the head).  Under this bracket should be a allen head type plug.  I wish I had a digital camera to show you what I mean.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Brandon86SE||09-26-2001||09:15 PM||||Ok, thanks Leadfoot for the advice, I'm just wondering now, how the heck to you work back there?!?!  I had to grab a CD (don't have a little mirror), and a flashlight.  I hung over top of the battery (with cover removed) banging my head on the decklid, and I think I saw what you were talking about, the battery plug is pretty well right by the spark plug wires, right?  If you're on here still, I'm gonna go into the chat.  A pic would still be mighty helpful for anyone with a cam!<P>Brandon||||reg||7
Z||000006||Leadfoot||09-27-2001||10:08 AM||||Too bad I missed you in the chat room.  The only real way to work that area between the firewall and engine is to take off the decklid.  Make sure the decklid straps are not broken (otherwise you will get polish air conditioning through the rear window....smack, shatter) and scribe the outline of the bracket to help in reinstalling the decklid.  For more room you could remove the dogbone and pull the engine back.  Good luck! ||||reg||1
Z||000007||maryjane||09-27-2001||01:18 PM||||I know it's in a bad place as far as being damaged if you run over something, but isn't there a drain plug in each of the coolant pipes running from the eng to the radiator? You would have to use the one going TO the radiator. Don't know if it is any closer or not, and might not show high temp if the wp or belt quit. Just a thought.<BR>After going out & looking, I realized the plugs are right on the bottom. never mind.Bad advice, sorry.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||09-28-2001||10:29 AM||||I just looked at my pile of 2.8 heads.  The plug (for the temp. sender hole) has a square recess, not an Allen (hex) recess.  The square recess measures approximately .31" across flats.  That means a standard socket drive (i.e. - 1/4" or 3/8") won't fit.<P>If I really wanted to get one of those plugs out, I would probably make a tool from one of my cheapie 3/8" extensions.  From one of the $5 socket sets, you know.<P>Grind the 3/8" square (male) end of the extension until it fits nicely in the square recess in the plug; then turn it with a suitable 3/8"-drive wrench.           ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Leadfoot||09-28-2001||08:49 PM||||Hey I was doing a search on PFF and I found a pic that shows the water plug on the head.  I is to the left of the dipstick.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011822.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011822.html</A> ||||reg||1
